\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Cleveland and McGill (1984b) used two different types of grouped bar charts: comparisons between adjacent bars, and comparisons between separated bars. It is widely acknowledged that comparisons between separated bars (Type 3 comparisons, in Cleveland \& McGill's terminology) are more difficult and error-prone.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Cleveland and McGill (1984b) used two different types of grouped bar charts: comparisons between adjacent bars, and comparisons between separated bars. It is widely acknowledged that comparisons between separated bars (Type 3 comparisons, in Cleveland \& McGill's terminology) are more difficult and error-prone.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces An illustration of the sine illusion (VanderPlas and Hofmann 2015), also known as the line-width illusion. All vertical lines are the same length, but the lines in the middle of the curve appear to be much shorter. The illusion results when implicit perceptual corrections useful for perceiving the size of objects with depth are applied to 2D stimuli with no actual depth. As 3D heuristics occasionally cause inaccurate communication when applied to 2D objects, it is reasonable to think that there might be some situations where charts making use of a realistic third dimension might be perceived more accurately than their 2D equivalents.\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Two dimensional, 3D digital rendering, and 3D-printed charts used in this study.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Two dimensional, 3D digital rendering, and 3D-printed charts used in this study.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Two dimensional, 3D digital rendering, and 3D-printed charts used in this study.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces A graphical representation of the study design. Only five of the seven ratios were used in each kit; at least one of the smallest or largest ratios were included along with 4 other charts; each selected ratio was displayed in all 3 mediums. For each medium \(\times \) ratio combination, the comparison type (separated or adjacent) was randomly determined. A total of 21 kits of 3D printed charts were created to include all combinations of the five ratios.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Demographic characteristics of participants in the study.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Screenshot of Shiny application practice screen. Three 2D bar charts with different ratios were provided, along with sliders indicating the correct proportion. Participants could practice with the sliders and preview the questions that would be asked as part of the task.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Screenshot of the applet collecting data for a 3D rendered chart task. Participants were asked to select which bar (circle or triangle) was smaller, and then to estimate the ratio of the smaller bar to the larger bar.\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Midmeans and observed values of log absolute errors for the true ratio of bars. Summary lines are computed from raw data using a loess smooth.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Predictions with standard errors for fixed effects in a generalized additive model with splines for ratio by comparison and ratio by display method. It is clear that the separated comparisons are easier to estimate when small, right around 50\%, or large, and harder to estimate between these points. What is interesting is that no such trend is present for adjacent comparisons.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Participant clicks on the WebGL interface (left) in order to interact with the plot. Most participants did not interact with the WebGL interface, perhaps because they did not realize that interacting was an option, but some participants utilized the interactivity heavily. (Right) Participant interactivity was not associated with increased accuracy.\relax }}{45}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Participant clicks on the WebGL interface (left) in order to interact with the plot. Most participants did not interact with the WebGL interface, perhaps because they did not realize that interacting was an option, but some participants utilized the interactivity heavily. (Right) Participant interactivity was not associated with increased accuracy.\relax }}{46}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Values for stimuli in the heatmap experiment. All values are paired with the constant stimuli of 50 units, creating nine pairs of stimuli.\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Mixture distribution of Equation\nobreakspace {}\ref {eq-random-z} using the formula for the top half of a sphere. As \(c\) approaches 1, the distribution resembles uniform random noise.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Placement of stimuli on the two data sets. Tiles with solid fills are the fixed stimuli values, where white tiles are randomly generated.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces 3D printed heatmaps.\relax }}{66}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces RGL rendering of Data Set 2 for 3dd charts.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Color palette for 2D-digital charts. The colors are interpolated from \#0C2841 to \#66D9FF, which are the colors of the lighting conditions for a 3D-printed chart created with cyan filament.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Graphics experiment page. Each trial contained two questions about a pair of stimuli, and a check box that displays the location of the stimuli if needed.\relax }}{69}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Histogram of experiment completion times. Online participants had generally shorter completion times due to a decreased number of trials.\relax }}{72}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Three potential estimation strategies from participants. Many participants followed instructions to estimate ratios. However, some participants appeared to estimate the difference between stimuli pairs or submitted random values.\relax }}{73}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Proportion of correct responses to Question 1 across stimuli pairs and media types. For all pairs, with the exception of Pair 5, a correct answer was `Larger value'. Stimuli Pair 5 had two identical values, meaning that options other than `Both values are the same' were incorrect.\relax }}{74}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces Distributions of log error overlayed with estimated marginal means and their 95\% confidence intervals. Differences were only detected between 2D heatmap and both types of 3D heatmaps, where the 2D heatmap had evidence of larger errors.\relax }}{75}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces Estimated marginal means of stimuli pair and dataset. Bars sharing the same letter are not significantly different from one another.\relax }}{76}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.13}{\ignorespaces Visual summary of time spent on each trial. 137 trials are omitted due to lasting longer than 60 seconds.\relax }}{77}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.14}{\ignorespaces Participant interactions with the digital 3D heatmaps.\relax }}{77}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.15}{\ignorespaces Participant interactions with the slider.\relax }}{78}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.16}{\ignorespaces Effect of initial slider position on the submitted slider value.\relax }}{78}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.17}{\ignorespaces Counts of response behavior for Stimuli Pair 5. This pair had identical values, which means that true solutions indicates marking that they were the same value and positioning the slider at 100.\relax }}{80}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.18}{\ignorespaces Interaction plots for media type and response filtering, facetted by stimuli pair and dataset.\relax }}{81}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.19}{\ignorespaces Interaction plots for dataset and response filtering, facetted by stimuli pair and media type.\relax }}{82}{figure.caption.60}%
